  "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=4\\,500.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+C{e}^{-(4\\,500\\cdot 0.2)t}}=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+C{e}^{-900t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=150$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\,500}{1+C{e}^{-900\\cdot 0}} & =150 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{4\\,500}{150} \\\\ C & =30-1 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+29{e}^{-900t}}.\n$$",
